INT. TAXIDERMIST'S WORKSHOP - NIGHT

Glass eyes in trays. A badger on a stand. A fox mid-leap, forever.

OLEKSANDR "SASHA" MELNYK, sixties, apron stiff with old plaster. Scalpel in hand. Steady.

RAFAELLA "RAFFI" COSTANZA, forties. Camel coat. Sits on a stool among the heads. Smiling.

RAFFI
You're late with the money, Sasha.

SASHA
I'm late with everything. Ask the owl.

RAFFI
The owl isn't my problem.

SASHA
The owl has been my problem six weeks. Every customer wants it perfect. Nobody wants to pay for perfect.

RAFFI
Neither do I.

SASHA
I told your brother in March. I said, give me till the autumn.

RAFFI
It's autumn.

SASHA
It's the end of autumn.

RAFFI
Sasha. Sasha. I like you. I like the shop. I like that nobody's been in here since Easter and the place still smells like a church.

SASHA
That's the formaldehyde.

RAFFI
That's what I said.

SASHA
You want the shop? Take it. You want the badger? Take the badger. But don't stand there telling me you like me.

RAFFI
I do like you.

SASHA
Then why are you in my chair?

She stands. Slowly. Pulls a long glass jar from the shelf and weighs it in her palm.

Sasha lifts the scalpel an inch.

RAFFI (CONT'D)
Put it down.

SASHA
You put it down.

The jar flies. Sasha ducks. Glass bursts on the badger. Raffi lunges across the bench and they crash into the shelves. Eyes spill across the floor, hundreds of them, rolling.

Raffi pins his wrist. He drives a knee into her ribs. She gasps. The scalpel skitters away under the fox.

They stop. Both breathing hard. Both looking at the dozens of little glass eyes staring up from the floor.

SASHA (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Now you've made a mess.
